{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the remaining camp shall escape</b> — In the plain sense, he said this in reference to 'perhaps,' for he said: perhaps one camp will be saved, for when he strikes one, the others will flee, or his wrath will subside, or salvation will come to them from HaShem. And so they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 76:3): Scripture teaches you proper conduct — a person should not place all his wealth in one corner. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 32:9), '<b>And the remaining camp shall escape</b>' — against his will — 'for I will fight him' — he prepared himself for three things: for prayer, for a gift, and for war. And I have seen in the Midrash (Ecclesiastes Rabbah 9:18): What did he do? He armed them from within and dressed them in white garments on the outside, and he prepared himself for three things. And this is the essential meaning. And the intention in this is that Jacob knows that not all of his seed will fall into the hand of Esav; if so, one camp will be saved in any event. And this also hints that the children of Esav will never decree upon us to wipe out our name, but they will do evils against some of us in some of their lands — one king among them decrees in his land against our property or against our persons, and another king is merciful in his place and saves the fugitives. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 76:3): 'If Esav comes to the one camp and strikes it' — these are our brethren in the South; 'and the remaining camp shall escape' — these are our brethren in the exile. See that this passage also hints for all generations."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The citation קה\"ר refers to Kohelet (Ecclesiastes) Rabbah. The abbreviation is resolved as קהלת רבה based on standard usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:25.814014583Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}